// =====================================================================
//  texish — Engine Features
//
//  THE flagship demo for the texish typesetting engine. It merges the
//  small engine-feature example scripts into one polished document that
//  exercises the CORE engine primitives: boxes and glue, framing,
//  balanced columns, box registers and \vsplit, cost-based page
//  breaking, vector pictures, verbatim text, syntax-highlighted code,
//  cross-references and a table of contents, and font/size switches.
//
//  The `document` package is used ONLY as the frame — for \section
//  headings, the title block, and paragraph indentation. Everything in
//  the body is a bare engine primitive, the subject of the demo.
//
//  Render with:  texish-cli scripts/texish-demo.script
// =====================================================================

\use{document}

\title {texish --- Engine Features}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\contents

\vskip 12pt

\section {Boxes and glue}

\label{sec:boxes}Everything texish sets is a \italic{box}, and the space between
boxes is \italic{glue} --- a length that can stretch and shrink. The engine builds
a paragraph by packing boxes and glue, then choosing line breaks. The primitives
below expose that machinery directly. This is \autoref{sec:boxes}.

\subsection {Horizontal and vertical boxes}

An \verb|\hbox| packs its content into a single line that never breaks; a
\verb|\vbox| stacks its content vertically. Here an \verb|\hbox| holds three
words with explicit glue between them, framed so its extent is visible:

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox{one\hskip 18pt two\hskip 18pt three}}

\subsection {Stretchable and shrinkable glue}

Glue is written \mono{natural plus stretch minus shrink}. Infinite stretch
(\mono{fil}, \mono{fill}) wins over any finite amount, so it pushes
neighbouring material apart. In the framed line below, the \verb|\hfil| between
the words absorbs all the slack equally, while \verb|\hfill| (a stronger
infinity) would take it all itself:

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox to:4in {left\hfil centre\hfil right}}

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox to:4in {pushed\hfill apart}}

A finite \mono{plus}/\mono{minus} glue flexes only as far as it is allowed;
here the gap may grow by up to 1in but is asked to fill 3in, so it stretches to
its limit and the box reports the rest as underfull (an engine, not a document,
concern):

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox to:3in {start\hskip 0pt plus 1in finish}}

\subsection {Kerns, rules, and shifted boxes}

A \verb|\kern| is rigid space that never flexes --- the spacing you reach for
when glue must not move. A \verb|\hrule| draws a horizontal rule across the box
it lands in, and \verb|\vrule| a vertical one. A box can be shifted off the
baseline with \verb|\raise| and \verb|\lower| while keeping its own width.

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox{A\kern 24pt B\kern 2pt C}}

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\hbox{base\raise 6pt \hbox{up} and \lower 4pt \hbox{down} again}}

\medskip
A vertical rule sits inline like any box: before\kern 3pt\vrule height:9pt depth:3pt\kern 3pt after.

\medskip
A horizontal rule, two points thick, drawn to an explicit width:

\medskip
\noindent\hrule width:3in ascent:1pt descent:1pt

\section {Framing boxes}

\label{sec:frame}The \verb|\fbox| and \verb|\framebox| primitives typeset their
body and draw a rectangular rule around it, padded by \verb|\fboxsep| and ruled
\verb|\fboxrule| thick. They flow inline like any other box. Their padding and
rule thickness are ordinary length variables, so they can be changed:

\medskip
\noindent A \fbox{plain frame}, then a \framebox{wider frame} after the rule was
thickened and the padding grown:

\medskip
\set fboxrule {1.5}
\set fboxsep {8}
\noindent\framebox{generous padding, thick rule}
\set fboxrule {0.4}
\set fboxsep {3}

\section {Balanced multi-column text}

\label{sec:columns}The \verb|\columns| primitive sets its body as several
balanced columns: the text is typeset once at the column measure, divided into
equal-height pieces with the same page-style breaking \verb|\vsplit| uses, and
the pieces are stood side by side. Three balanced columns:

\columns{3}{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Setting a paragraph in narrow columns
forces many short lines, which is exactly what makes column balancing visible:
the engine can choose a break between almost any pair of lines, so it divides the
galley into three pieces of nearly equal height. The reader's eye travels down
the first column, back to the top of the second, and down again, the way a
newspaper or a two-column paper is read.}

\vskip 12pt

\noindent The same material in two columns with a wider gutter, set by the
optional \mono{gap} parameter:

\columns gap:32 {2}{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Two columns leave longer lines than
three, so the texture is closer to ordinary text while still balancing the
material into two level halves.}

\section {Box registers and vsplit}

\label{sec:vsplit}A box can be saved in a named register with \verb|\setbox| and
recalled with \verb|\box|. The \verb|\vsplit| primitive cuts a saved vertical box
at a page-style breakpoint --- the latest legal break no taller than a target
height --- handing back the top piece and leaving the remainder in the register.
It is the building block under split footnotes and column balancing. Below a tall
paragraph is set into a narrow \verb|\vbox|, split near its middle, and the two
halves are stood side by side.

\set hsize {170}
\setbox col \vbox{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Typesetting a paragraph this narrow
forces many short lines, which gives the column builder plenty of legal
breakpoints --- one between every pair of lines --- so a split can land almost
anywhere. Everything above the chosen break becomes the top piece and everything
below it stays behind, ready to flow into the next column or page.}
\set hsize {460}

\vskip 6pt

\set colheight {\ht col}
\setbox colA \vsplit col to:{\calc{\colheight / 2}}

\noindent\hbox{\box colA \hskip 24 \box col}

\section {Cost-based page breaking}

\label{sec:pages}The page builder chooses where to break by cost, as TeX does,
rather than simply taking the latest legal break that fits. The cost is the
page's badness there --- how hard its glue must work to reach the page height ---
plus the \verb|\penalty| at the break. A positive penalty makes a discouraged
break less attractive; \verb|\nobreak| forbids one outright; \verb|\eject| forces
one. On an ordinary rigid page every break that fits is equally good, so the rule
reduces to the familiar last-fit behaviour; the difference shows only where the
glue can flex or a penalty intervenes.

\penalty {9000}

A penalty of nine thousand sits just before this paragraph. It is not an absolute
prohibition --- that would be ten thousand --- but it is costly enough that the
page builder prefers to break earlier and carry this paragraph over whole, rather
than split the page right above it. That is the fine control cost-based breaking
gives the author over the rhythm of the pages.

\eject

\section {Pictures --- vector graphics}

\label{sec:picture}A \verb|\picture| is a fixed-size box drawn into with shapes,
paths, and placed type. It flows in the text like any other box and prints through
the same backend as the page itself. Coordinates are y-up from the lower-left; a
bare number is a point and a unit suffix (\mono{in}, \mono{mm}, \mono{pt})
is honoured. The pen is set with \verb|\stroke|, the fill with \verb|\fill|.

\medskip
\noindent\picture width:4in height:1.6in {
  \fill{lightsteelblue} \stroke{steelblue} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \rect{0.2in 0.2in 1in 1in}
  \circle{2in 0.7in 0.5in}
  \nofill \stroke{firebrick}
  \ellipse{3.2in 0.7in 0.6in 0.4in}
  \fill{seagreen} \nostroke
  \polygon{0.2in 1.4in  0.7in 1.4in  0.45in 0.2in}
}

A \verb|\path| builds a freeform outline from move, line, and curve segments. A
filled Bézier wave with a stroked edge:

\medskip
\noindent\picture width:4in height:1.4in {
  \fill{papayawhip} \stroke{chocolate} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \path{
    \moveto{0.2in 0.2in}
    \lineto{0.2in 0.8in}
    \curveto{1.2in 1.6in  2.6in -0.4in  3.8in 0.8in}
    \lineto{3.8in 0.2in}
    \close
  }
}

Because texish has loops and arithmetic, a plot is just a path built in a
\verb|\for|. The axes are lines and the curve samples $y = x^2$; \verb|\at| drops
typeset content --- here math --- at a coordinate with an anchor.

\medskip
\noindent\picture width:3in height:2.4in {
  \stroke{gray} \linewidth{1pt}
  \line{0 0  2.8in 0}
  \line{0 0  0 2.2in}
  \stroke{royalblue} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \path{
    \moveto{0 0}
    \for\i{\range{1}{14}}{
      \lineto{\*{\i}{14}  \*{\*{\i}{\i}}{0.8}}
    }
  }
  \at anchor:south {1.4in -0.1in}{$x$}
  \at anchor:west {0.05in 2.1in}{$y = x^2$}
}

\eject

\section {Verbatim text}

\label{sec:verbatim}Inline verbatim is set with \verb|\verb|. The character right
after it is the delimiter, and the text runs to that character's next occurrence,
set literally in the typewriter face. So \verb|x = a_b + c| and a URL like
\verb|http://example.com/path| keep every character exactly as written, with no
comment, escape, or active character given its usual meaning. Pick a delimiter the
text does not use: \verb#a|b|c# uses a hash because the text contains bars.

The verbatim environment sets a whole block the same way --- one source line per
output line, leading spaces preserved, no line breaking:

\begin{verbatim}
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)   // a // here is literal, not a comment

print(factorial(5))
\end{verbatim}

\section {Syntax-highlighted code}

\label{sec:code}Source code is set with \verb|\code| in the code face. A bare
\verb|\code{…}| sets the body plainly; a \verb|\code[language]{…}| highlights it
with that language's grammar and the current theme (a light theme by default, so
the ink reads on white paper). A short JSON value as a highlighted block:

\code[json]{{"name": "texish", "version": 5, "cross": ["jvm", "js", "native"]}}

For a whole listing --- and for code whose braces do not balance --- the code
environment reads everything up to its end and takes its language from
\verb|\set codelang|:

\set codelang {scala}
\begin{code}
def quadratic(a: Double, b: Double, c: Double) =
  val disc = b * b - 4 * a * c     // the discriminant
  if disc < 0 then None
  else
    val r = math.sqrt(disc)
    Some((-b + r) / (2 * a), (-b - r) / (2 * a))
\end{code}

The theme is selectable with \verb|\set codetheme|; here a Python snippet under a
different light theme:

\set codetheme {gruvbox-light}
\code[python]{def greet(name):
    print(f"hello, {name}")   # an f-string
    return len(name)}

\section {Named counters}

\label{sec:counters}Every numbered thing in a document — a section, a figure, a
footnote — rides on a \italic{counter}: a named integer the engine holds and advances
for you. \verb|\newcounter| declares one (starting at zero), \verb|\stepcounter|
adds one, \verb|\setcounter| and \verb|\addtocounter| assign and adjust it, and
\verb|\value| reads it back so a formatter can render it. The six formatters turn
the same value into different text:

\newcounter{demo}
\setcounter{demo}{4}
\begin{quote}
\noindent\verb|\arabic| \arabic{\value{demo}}, \verb|\roman| \roman{\value{demo}},
\verb|\Roman| \Roman{\value{demo}}, \verb|\alph| \alph{\value{demo}}, \verb|\Alph|
\Alph{\value{demo}}, \verb|\fnsymbol| \fnsymbol{\value{demo}}.
\end{quote}

The feature that makes counters more than variables is \verb|\counterwithin|, which
binds a child counter under a parent so that stepping the parent resets the child to
zero — LaTeX's reset list, and exactly what makes a figure number restart in each
new chapter. Here \verb|fig| is bound under \verb|chap|, and the figure number falls
back to one each time the chapter advances:

\newcounter{chap}
\newcounter{fig}
\counterwithin{fig}{chap}
\setcounter{chap}{0}
\begin{quote}
\noindent
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{fig}\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig}\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig}\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig}}\quad
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{fig}\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig}\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig}}
\end{quote}

\noindent The reset is recursive, so a three-level chain (part, chapter, section)
cascades from a single step of the outermost counter — the same machinery the
numbered headings in this very document run on.

\section {Cross-references and the table of contents}

\label{sec:refs}This document is typeset more than once. On the first pass the
engine learns where every numbered heading lands; on the next it fills in the
table of contents at the top and the references here. A \verb|\label| binds a name
to a numbered thing; \verb|\ref| names its number, \verb|\pageref| its page, and
\verb|\autoref| the number with its kind word in front.

For example, the boxes-and-glue material is \autoref{sec:boxes} beginning on
page~\pageref{sec:boxes}; pictures are \autoref{sec:picture} on
page~\pageref{sec:picture}; and this very section is \autoref{sec:refs} on
page~\pageref{sec:refs}. Forward references work the same way: the conclusion is
\autoref{sec:fonts}, on page~\pageref{sec:fonts}. None of those numbers was known
when the text was written --- they were filled in by re-running the document over
one shared reference table, which is also what draws the dotted leaders in the
contents above.

\section {Fonts and sizes}

\label{sec:fonts}The active face is chosen with \verb|\font typeface size style|,
and the size alone with \verb|\fontsize|, which keeps the current shape and series.
Shape switches like \verb|\bold| and \verb|\italic| compose with whatever size is
in force. This is \autoref{sec:fonts}, the last section.

\medskip
\noindent A size sweep, each set with \verb|\fontsize| inside its own group so the
surrounding text is unaffected:
{\fontsize{8} eight}, {\fontsize{12} twelve}, {\fontsize{18} eighteen}, and
{\fontsize{24} twenty-four} points.

\medskip
\noindent Shape switches compose with size: {\fontsize{16} {\bold{bold}} and
{\italic{italic}} at sixteen points}, then back to the body size and shape for the
rest of the line.

\medskip
\noindent A full \verb|\font| selection picks typeface, size, and style at once:
{\font lmroman 14 bold a fourteen-point bold run} that reverts at the closing
brace.

\vfill\eject
